  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "offensive five lineup composition",
    "full five-man substitution",
    "shot mix",
    "play type distribution",
    "personnel effects",
    "lineup change detection"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to isolate offensive lineup effects on shot mix while controlling for defensive matchups and game context?",
    "What is the frequency of full five-man offensive substitutions in NBA games?",
    "How to define shot mix from play-by-play data?",
    "How to handle small sample sizes for rare lineups?",
    "What statistical tests can compare shot distributions between different lineups?",
    "How to account for confounding due to opponent personnel and game situation?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF34",
      "status_as_recorded": "inconclusive",
      "finding": "Offenses exhibit measurable shot mix changes when the opponent lacks a rim big (DRIVE +0.82pp, POST_UP +0.43pp, 3PA -1.79pp), but rim share and overall points per possession (PPP +0.008, t=+1.1) do not significantly change, leading to a powered-null verdict. The study used 1.07M possessions and within-game fixed effects, showing the design can detect personnel-driven shot mix differences, albeit with weak effects on efficiency."
    }
  ],
  "failed_approaches": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF34",
      "what_failed": "Attempt to detect opponent lineup adaptation effects on shot mix and efficiency, despite >1M possessions, produced only weak play-type shifts and no significant PPP change, failing to meet publication-worthy standards.",
      "why": "Effect sizes were small (≤1pp per play type) and the primary outcome (PPP) was null, leaving the overall finding WEAK/SMALL; the design could not demonstrate a practically meaningful efficiency effect even with large N."
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "Opponent lineup changes (removing a rim protector) induce detectable shifts in shot mix (drive and three-point rates) using play-by-play data and within-game fixed effects, confirming that the data infrastructure can capture personnel-driven shot selection changes."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "Whether full offensive five substitutions produce analogous shifts in own shot mix.",
    "The magnitude and statistical significance of shot mix changes when the offensive lineup changes en bloc.",
    "Adequate power to detect such changes after controlling for confounding game-state variables (end of quarter, score margin).",
    "The prevalence and sample size of full five-man substitution events in the three-season dataset."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "Full lineup changes likely shift shot mix because different player combinations have distinct shot profiles, but the effect size may be comparable to the small shifts seen in FAM:SF34.",
    "Pooling all full five-man substitutions across three seasons should yield a large enough sample to detect moderate play-type shifts, but a definitive efficiency effect (PPP) is unlikely.",
    "The research question as stated ('showing the design can detect personnel effects') is primarily a methodological validation rather than a substantive basketball hypothesis; success would be a statistically significant difference in shot mix distribution.",
    "Confounding by game situation (garbage time, end-of-quarter heaves) and regression to the mean (bad lineups replaced) must be rigorously addressed with control variables and a falsification test."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "Empirical frequency of clean full five-man offensive substitutions (no overlapping opponent changes) across three seasons.",
    "Effect size required for meaningful methodological validation (what constitutes a successful detection).",
    "Appropriate threshold for statistical significance given multiple play-type comparisons.",
    "Degree to which opponent lineup changes occur simultaneously with offensive five changes, potentially biasing estimates.",
    "Practical significance of detecting any nonzero shot mix shift when overall efficiency is unchanged."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "no",
    "explanation": "The retrieved evidence did not directly answer the current question but provided crucial feasibility information: shot mix shifts from personnel changes are measurable with the same data, though small and not always supported by efficiency outcomes. This confirms the data's suitability and highlights the need for large samples and careful control of confounding, informing the design but not altering the decision to proceed with changes."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.7,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "The number of clean full five-man offensive substitutions may be limited, and effect sizes for shot mix shifts are likely small, requiring a large sample to achieve adequate power. A pilot count from the data is needed to confirm feasibility."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "Full lineup changes often occur at natural breaks (end of quarter, after a poor run) or in garbage time, potentially conflating shot mix changes with game situation, opponent quality, or mean reversion. Within-game fixed effects, score margin controls, and prior performance adjustments are necessary."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "partly",
    "cited": ["FAM:SF34"],
    "summary": "Previous work (FAM:SF34) showed that opponent personnel changes (removing a rim big) cause detectable but weak shot mix shifts, establishing that the data and play-type metrics can capture lineup-induced effects, but did not study offensive five substitutions. The question therefore builds on existing methodological capability but has not been directly tested."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "Uncertain event count; if full five-man substitutions are too rare, the study may lack power to detect small-to-moderate shot mix differences."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Game-state confounders (end of quarter, blowout substitutions) could produce spurious shot mix changes that mimic or mask true personnel effects."
    },
    {
      "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
      "text": "Teams may substitute all five players after an unusually poor offensive stretch, leading to regression-to-the-mean in shot quality that could be mistaken for a lineup effect."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "Analyzing many play-type categories simultaneously increases the risk of false positive findings unless multiplicity correction is applied."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "Individual five-man lineups have small sample sizes, but this can be mitigated by pooling all full-substitution events and using within-game contrasts rather than lineup-level comparisons."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.3,
    "rejected": 0.2,
    "untestable": 0.1,
    "artifact": 0.05,
    "uncertain": 0.35
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The average within-game change in offensive shot mix (distribution of play types) immediately after a full five-man substitution, compared to the shots taken in the preceding possessions, after adjusting for period, clock, score margin, opponent lineup, and recent offensive performance.",
    "unit": "A single possession, classified into mutually exclusive play types (e.g., isolation, pick-and-roll ball-handler, pick-and-roll roll man, spot-up, transition, cut, post-up, other) based on the derived play-type possession records.",
    "sample": "All regular season and playoff games from the 2022-23, 2023-24, and 2024-25 seasons. An event is defined as a dead-ball substitution where exactly five offensive players are replaced simultaneously. For each event, we extract up to 10 possessions before and 10 possessions after, excluding possessions that contain another substitution. Only events with at least 5 possessions on each side are retained. The opponent lineup is held constant in a subset for a secondary analysis.",
    "power": "Prior to full analysis, conduct a pilot on the 2022-23 season to (a) count qualifying events, (b) estimate the variance of shot mix shifts under the null, and (c) compute minimum detectable effect size in terms of a chi-square non-centrality parameter. Full analysis proceeds only if there are at least 400 events, providing >80% power to detect a small-to-medium shift (Cohen's w = 0.15) at α=0.05.",
    "controls": "Fixed effects for each game × quarter combination; linear controls for half-minute clock bucket, score margin, and a rolling 10-possession offensive points per possession. Additionally, a categorical control for opponent lineup configuration (starter/heavy bench/hybrid) if a direct opponent-lineup match is unavailable.",
    "falsification": "Perform the identical analysis on substitutions where only four offensive players are changed. A smaller or no detectable shift in shot mix for four-man changes would support the interpretation that the full-five effect is driven by complete personnel turnover rather than general substitution noise. Also conduct a placebo test using events re-centred at a random clock time with no actual substitution.",
    "multiplicity": "Primary hypothesis test: omnibus chi-square test of independence between pre/post period and play type distribution. Secondary tests: individual play-type proportions tested with Fisher's exact tests and Bonferroni correction. Report both raw and adjusted p-values.",
    "decision_rule": "The design is considered validated (supporting the question) if the omnibus chi-square test rejects the null of equal pre- and post-substitution shot mix distributions at p < 0.05, and the effect size (Cramér's V) exceeds 0.10. Otherwise, the study concludes that the design cannot reliably detect personnel effects from this natural experiment.",
    "data_version": "NBA play-by-play and derived play-type possession database as of 2026-11-02 (2022-23 through 2024-25 complete seasons)."
  }
}

